<resources>

    <style name="AppTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:editTextBackground">@drawable/rn_edit_text_material</item>
        <!-- The app draws light chrome, so the system bars are forced light
             to match. Without this the status bar text is invisible against
             the light background during the pre-JS launch window. -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">#F4F5F7</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">#FFFFFF</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">true</item>
    </style>

    <!--
      The launch theme. Android 12+ always draws a splash from the launcher
      icon; this replaces the default grey-on-white version with the brand
      mark, and hands over to AppTheme once the activity is up.
      postSplashScreenTheme is what performs that handover — without it the
      app keeps the splash theme and loses AppTheme's system bar colours.

      The background is the app's own background colour rather than the brand
      navy. The splash is followed immediately by a light app, and a navy
      launch window meant inverting the system bars for a few frames and then
      flipping them back — a visible flash on every cold start. Matching the
      app means there is nothing to flip and no flash to see.

      windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor is deliberately NOT set: it only
      exists from Android 12, and the navy disc behind the mark is drawn into
      ic_splash_logo instead so every supported version looks the same.
    -->
    <style name="AppTheme.Splash" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/splash_background</item>
        <!--
          Deliberately a STATIC mark, and deliberately not held open.

          This window exists only to cover the gap before React Native can
          draw; the branded splash is components/SplashOverlay, which can do
          what this one fundamentally cannot — show the name and a tagline.

          An animated icon here was worse than useless. Two splashes each
          running their own animation have to hand over mid-motion, and
          every mismatch in timing or position between them showed as a jump
          or a ghosted double image. One still frame handing over to an
          identical still frame cannot glitch.
        -->
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_splash_logo</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/AppTheme</item>
        <!-- Identical to AppTheme's bars, so the handover changes nothing. -->
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@color/splash_background</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">true</item>
    </style>

</resources>
